# Counter-example: merge their new history into yours.
#
# The merge resolves, the files come out right, and the branch now contains
# both their squashed commit AND the two originals it replaced -- every change
# of theirs applied twice, joined by a merge commit. Pushing it puts the
# history they just deleted back on the remote.

git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
git merge --no-edit origin/feature/webhooks
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
